program            = 002_typedef_and_scope
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Typedef declaration and use, block scope, and shadowing through the tag, member and label namespaces
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every object is fully written before it is read. The four members of the block-local struct
point object deep are all assigned before the block computes tag_inner from them. The struct
holder object h has both members assigned before either is printed. Both sibling blocks
assign their local before copying it out, and both destinations sibling_first and
sibling_second are written before use. The block-local short object narrow is initialized at
its declaration. All arithmetic is on small int and short values far from the range edges,
so there is no signed overflow: the largest intermediate value is 26. No shift is performed
and no pointer arithmetic is performed. The goto jumps forward to a label in the same
function and the same block, crossing no variably modified declaration, so no object is
skipped over. No object is modified twice between sequence points and no printf call
receives more than one side-effecting argument. There is no aliasing violation: every object
is accessed only through an lvalue of its own declared type, and the two struct point types
are never mixed because the inner tag is visible only inside its own block. No padding byte
is read: only named members are accessed.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every printed size is of an int-based or short-based type only. The outer sizeof(struct
point) is two ints (8 bytes), the inner sizeof deep is four ints (16 bytes) and
sizeof(small_t) is one short (2 bytes). All three are identical on x86_64, i686, aarch64 and
riscv64, because int is 4 bytes and short is 2 bytes on all four. No sizeof of long,
pointer, double, long long or any aggregate containing them is printed, so the measured i686
difference (4-byte long and pointer versus 8 bytes elsewhere) cannot surface. No address or
pointer value is printed. Plain char is not used, so the measured char signedness difference
(signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) is irrelevant. The only printf
conversion specifier used in the program is %d.

Shadowing technique note: -Wshadow is part of the mandated default warning gate and is not
waived for this area, and it rejects ordinary-identifier shadowing outright. Shadowing is
therefore exercised in the namespaces -Wshadow does not police -- the tag namespace (a
block-local struct point hiding the file-scope one), the member namespace (struct holder has
a member named width alongside a file-scope int width) and the label namespace (a label
spelled width) -- together with two disjoint sibling blocks that prove block scope without
nesting. The feature is tested rather than excluded, and the gate is not relaxed.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
tag_scope=8 26
member_ns=11 100
typedef_scope=7 11
sibling_blocks=1 2
label_ns=1
END
